  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2 over x, z from −2 to 2, drawn at 0.3 × loss; steeper along x (across the screen). Steady descent needs learning rate under 0.5; at 0.5 x swings back and forth, above it the swings grow.",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise is Gaussian with spread 3·√((64−B)/(63·B)) per setting: 3 at B = 1, exactly 0 at B = 64. Noise follows a fixed seed per run and step, so Reset replays it.",
    "Both arrows show steps (learning rate × slope estimate), not the slope itself, drawn at 2× their true length. The purple one is where the ball goes next; the dark one is the full-data step. The length readout is the true step length.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 10, the highest loss on the drawn bowl (diverged). Points off the bowl are drawn at its rim; the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The trail holds the start plus one point per step (up to 41 points); the ghost keeps the previous run's path. With noise, single steps can raise the loss; it falls only on average."
  ]
}